select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 12, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.0", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.0 Overview", "body": "Bachelier, L. (1900). Théorie de la spéculation. Annales Scientifiques de l'École Normale Supérieure, 17, 21–86.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapters 14–15.\nItô, K. (1951). On stochastic differential equations. Memoirs of the American Mathematical Society, 4, 1–51.\nNeftci, S. N. (2000). An Introduction to the Mathematics of Financial Derivatives (2nd ed.). Academic Press.\nSamuelson, P. A. (1965). Rational theory of warrant pricing. Industrial Management Review, 6(2), 13–31."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.0", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nIs the call cheap or dear?\nA three-month European call on Northwind shares with a strike of $30 trades at $1.52. The share price is $28 and the risk-free rate is 5 percent. A colleague who has studied the company states that the volatility of its shares is 25 percent per year. If the colleague is right, how should the market price of the call be regarded?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.1", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.1", "heading": "Assumptions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.1 Assumptions", "body": "The model of Fischer Black, Myron Scholes, and Robert Merton values a European option on a share by the argument of Chapter 10, applied to a share price that changes continuously and not in steps. It rests on the following assumptions.\nThe share price follows the geometric Brownian motion of Result 11.1, with constant expected return \\(\\mu\\) and constant volatility \\(\\sigma\\).\nShort selling is permitted, and the proceeds are available in full.\nThere are no transaction costs or taxes, and securities are perfectly divisible.\nThe share pays no dividends during the life of the option.\nThere are no riskless arbitrage opportunities.\nTrading in securities is continuous.\nThe risk-free rate \\(r\\) is constant and the same for all maturities.\nSeveral of these can be relaxed. Section 12.7 allows for dividends; the rate \\(r\\) may be a known function of time; and the volatility may be a known function of time, in which case \\(\\sigma^{2}\\) in the formulas is replaced by its average over the life of the option. The first assumption is the one of substance. Chapter 15 examines the evidence against it and the adjustments that practitioners make."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.2#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.2", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.2", "heading": "The idea of the model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.2 The idea of the model", "body": "Chapter 10 showed that on a binomial tree an option can be replicated by a portfolio of \\(\\Delta\\) shares and risk-free borrowing, adjusted at each node. The Black–Scholes–Merton argument is the same, with adjustment at every instant. Over a very short interval the price of an option and the price of the share are perfectly correlated, because both are driven by the single source of uncertainty in the share price. A portfolio that is short one option and long the appropriate number of shares is therefore riskless over that interval and must earn the risk-free rate. This single requirement determines the price of the option.\nTwo consequences should be noted before the formula is stated. The expected return on the share, \\(\\mu\\), does not appear in the price of the option, for the reason given in Section 10.2: it is already reflected in the price of the share. And because \\(\\mu\\) does not appear, the option may be valued by the principle of risk-neutral valuation, Principle 10.1: assume that the share is expected to earn the risk-free rate, compute the expected payoff, and discount at the risk-free rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.3#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.3", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.3", "heading": "★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.3 ★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "body": "Optional section. This section requires Itô's lemma, Result 11.3. Readers who omitted Section 11.6 may proceed to Section 12.4.\nLet \\(f\\) be the price of a derivative that depends on the share price \\(S\\) and on time \\(t\\). The share price follows \\(dS=\\mu S\\,dt+\\sigma S\\,dz\\). By Itô's lemma,\n\\[ df=\\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}\\mu S+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}+\\frac12\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right)dt+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}\\sigma S\\,dz . \\]\nForm a portfolio that is short one derivative and long \\(\\partial f/\\partial S\\) shares. Its value is\n\\[ \\Pi=-f+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}S, \\]\nand the change in its value over a short interval is \\(d\\Pi=-df+(\\partial f/\\partial S)\\,dS\\). Substituting the two processes, the terms in \\(dz\\) cancel, and so do the terms in \\(\\mu\\):\n\\[ d\\Pi=\\left(-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}-\\frac12\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right)dt . \\]\nThe portfolio is riskless over the interval, and in the absence of arbitrage it must earn the risk-free rate: \\(d\\Pi=r\\Pi\\,dt\\). Hence"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.3#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.3", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.3", "heading": "★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.3 ★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "body": "\\[ \\left(-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}-\\frac12\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\right)dt=r\\left(-f+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}S\\right)dt, \\]\nwhich rearranges to the following.\nResult 12.1 — The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation\n\\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}+rS\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial S}+\\frac12\\sigma^{2}S^{2}\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial S^{2}}=rf . \\]\nThe equation holds for every derivative whose value depends only on the price of a share that pays no dividend and on time. Different derivatives are distinguished by their boundary conditions, which specify the value at the boundaries of the possible values of \\(S\\) and \\(t\\). For a European call the condition is \\(f=\\max(S-K,0)\\) when \\(t=T\\); for a European put, \\(f=\\max(K-S,0)\\) when \\(t=T\\). The simplest solution is that for a forward contract with delivery price \\(K\\), \\(f=S-Ke^{-r(T-t)}\\) from Section 4.5: here \\(\\partial f/\\partial t=-rKe^{-r(T-t)}\\), \\(\\partial f/\\partial S=1\\), and \\(\\partial^{2}f/\\partial S^{2}=0\\), so the left-hand side is \\(-rKe^{-r(T-t)}+rS=rf\\), as required."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.3#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.3", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.3", "heading": "★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.3 ★ The Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation", "body": "The portfolio is riskless only for an instant. As \\(S\\) and \\(t\\) change, so does \\(\\partial f/\\partial S\\), and the number of shares held must be adjusted continually. The equation contains \\(S\\), \\(t\\), \\(\\sigma\\), and \\(r\\), none of which depends on the risk preferences of investors. The expected return \\(\\mu\\), which does depend on them, has disappeared. This is the formal justification of risk-neutral valuation."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "The pricing formulas", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 The pricing formulas", "body": "Result 12.2 — The Black–Scholes–Merton formulas\nThe prices of a European call and a European put on a share that pays no dividend are \\[ c=S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2), \\qquad p=Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2)-S_0N(-d_1), \\] where \\[ d_1=\\frac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r+\\sigma^{2}/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T}}, \\qquad d_2=d_1-\\sigma\\sqrt{T}, \\] and \\(N(x)\\) is the probability that a variable with a standard normal distribution is less than \\(x\\).\nThe function \\(N(x)\\) is the cumulative standard normal distribution. It is available in every spreadsheet and statistical program; a few values are given in Table 12.1. By symmetry, \\(N(-x)=1-N(x)\\).\nTable 12.1 Selected values of the cumulative standard normal distribution\n\\(x\\)−2.0−1.5−1.0−0.500.51.01.52.0\n\\(N(x)\\)0.02280.06680.15870.30850.50000.69150.84130.93320.9772\nThe two formulas are consistent with put–call parity. Substituting \\(N(-d_1)=1-N(d_1)\\) and \\(N(-d_2)=1-N(d_2)\\) in the formula for the put gives \\(p=c+Ke^{-rT}-S_0\\), which is Result 8.2. The formulas satisfy the differential equation of Result 12.1 and its boundary conditions, and they are the limit of the binomial value of Chapter 10 as the number of steps increases, as Interactive figure 10.2 showed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "The pricing formulas", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 The pricing formulas", "body": "Worked example 12.1 — The Northwind options\nNorthwind shares trade at $28 and have a volatility of 39 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Find the prices of three-month European options with a strike of $30.\n1\nPreliminaries. \\(\\ln(28/30)=-0.06899\\); \\(\\sigma^{2}/2=0.07605\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt{T}=0.39\\times0.5=0.195\\); \\(Ke^{-rT}=30e^{-0.0125}=29.6273\\).\n2\n\\(d_1\\). \\(\\dfrac{-0.06899+(0.05+0.07605)\\times0.25}{0.195}=\\dfrac{-0.06899+0.03151}{0.195}=-0.1922\\).\n3\n\\(d_2\\). \\(-0.1922-0.195=-0.3872\\).\n4\nNormal probabilities. \\(N(-0.1922)=0.4238\\); \\(N(-0.3872)=0.3493\\).\n5\nCall. \\(c=28\\times0.4238-29.6273\\times0.3493=11.866-10.349=\\$1.52\\).\n6\nPut. \\(p=29.6273\\times(1-0.3493)-28\\times(1-0.4238)=19.278-16.134=\\$3.14\\). These are the prices of Table 9.1, and \\(1.52+29.63=3.14+28.00\\) to within rounding."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "Interpretation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 Interpretation", "body": "Each term of the formula for the call has a meaning. The call pays \\(S_T-K\\) if \\(S_T>K\\) and nothing otherwise. Its value is the discounted risk-neutral expected value of this payoff, which may be separated into the amount received and the amount paid.\n\\(N(d_2)\\) is the probability, in a risk-neutral world, that the option will be exercised, that is, that \\(S_T>K\\). The term \\(Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)\\) is the present value of the strike price multiplied by the probability that it will be paid.\n\\(S_0N(d_1)\\) is the present value, in a risk-neutral world, of receiving the share if the option is exercised and nothing otherwise. It exceeds \\(S_0N(d_2)\\) because, in the outcomes in which the option is exercised, the share is worth more than average.\n\\(N(d_1)\\) is also the delta of the call: the number of shares in the replicating portfolio of Chapter 10. The formula states that a call is equivalent to \\(N(d_1)\\) shares financed by borrowing \\(Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)\\). In Worked example 12.1 the call is replicated by 0.4238 shares, worth $11.87, and borrowing of $10.35."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "Interpretation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 Interpretation", "body": "The formula behaves sensibly at its limits. When the share price is very large relative to the strike, \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) are large, both probabilities approach 1, and \\(c\\) approaches \\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\), the value of a forward contract: exercise is nearly certain. When the volatility approaches zero, the share grows at the risk-free rate with certainty, and the call is worth \\(\\max(S_0-Ke^{-rT},0)\\), the lower bound of Result 8.1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "Interpretation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 Interpretation", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 12.1 — The Black–Scholes–Merton calculator\nFrom five inputs to the prices of a call and a put\nSet the five inputs. The table carries out the calculation of Worked example 12.1 step by step, and the figure shows the values of the call and the put across share prices. Change one input at a time and confirm each row of Table 8.1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.4#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.4", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.4", "heading": "Interpretation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.4 Interpretation", "body": "Concept check 12.1\nFor a European call, \\(d_1=0.60\\) and \\(d_2=0.40\\), so that \\(N(d_1)=0.7257\\) and \\(N(d_2)=0.6554\\). Which statement is correct?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.5#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.5", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.5", "heading": "★ The formula as a risk-neutral expectation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.5 ★ The formula as a risk-neutral expectation", "body": "Optional section. This section outlines the derivation of the formula for the call from Principle 10.1 and Result 11.2.\nIn a risk-neutral world the expected return on the share is \\(r\\), so that by Result 11.2 \\(\\ln S_T\\) is normal with mean \\(m=\\ln S_0+(r-\\sigma^{2}/2)T\\) and standard deviation \\(s=\\sigma\\sqrt{T}\\). The value of the call is \\(c=e^{-rT}\\hat E[\\max(S_T-K,0)]\\), where \\(\\hat E\\) denotes the expectation in that world. Write the expectation as \\(\\hat E[S_T\\,\\mathbf 1_{S_T>K}]-K\\,\\hat P(S_T>K)\\), where \\(\\mathbf 1\\) equals 1 when the condition holds and 0 otherwise.\nSecond term. \\(\\hat P(S_T>K)=\\hat P(\\ln S_T>\\ln K)=N\\!\\left(\\dfrac{m-\\ln K}{s}\\right)\\), and \\(\\dfrac{m-\\ln K}{s}=\\dfrac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-\\sigma^{2}/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}=d_2\\).\nFirst term. For a lognormal variable, a standard integration (completing the square in the exponent) gives \\(\\hat E[S_T\\,\\mathbf 1_{S_T>K}]=e^{m+s^{2}/2}\\,N\\!\\left(\\dfrac{m+s^{2}-\\ln K}{s}\\right)\\). Here \\(e^{m+s^{2}/2}=S_0e^{rT}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{m+s^{2}-\\ln K}{s}=d_2+\\sigma\\sqrt T=d_1\\).\nCombining the two, \\(c=e^{-rT}\\left[S_0e^{rT}N(d_1)-KN(d_2)\\right]=S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "Of the five inputs to the formula, four can be observed: the share price, the strike, the time to expiration, and the risk-free rate. The fifth, the volatility of the share over the life of the option, cannot. Two approaches are used.\nHistorical volatility is the estimate obtained from past prices by the method of Section 11.7. It describes the past, and its use assumes that the future will resemble it.\nImplied volatility is the volatility that, when substituted into the formula, gives the price at which the option trades in the market. It is found by reversing the usual calculation: the price is known and the volatility is the unknown. The formula cannot be inverted algebraically, but because the price of an option increases steadily with volatility, the implied volatility is easily found by trial. The simplest systematic method is bisection: choose a low volatility and a high one whose model prices lie on either side of the market price; test the midpoint; discard the half of the interval that cannot contain the answer; and repeat."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "example", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "Worked example 12.2 — An implied volatility\nThe Northwind call of Worked example 12.1 trades at $1.80 and not at $1.52. Find the implied volatility to the nearest percentage point.\n1\nBracket. At \\(\\sigma=40\\%\\) the formula gives $1.572, which is too low; at \\(\\sigma=50\\%\\) it gives $2.125, which is too high. The implied volatility lies between 40 and 50 percent.\n2\nMidpoint. At \\(\\sigma=45\\%\\) the formula gives $1.848, which is too high. The answer lies between 40 and 45 percent.\n3\nContinue. At 42.5 percent: $1.710, too low. At 43.75 percent: $1.779, too low. At 44.4 percent: $1.815, too high.\n4\nResult. The implied volatility is 44 percent; to two decimal places it is 44.14 percent."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "Implied volatility is the market's assessment of the future volatility of the share, as expressed in the price that traders are willing to pay for options. It is forward-looking where historical volatility is backward-looking, and traders use it as the unit in which option prices are compared. A price of $1.52 for one option and $4.84 for another conveys little; implied volatilities of 39 percent for both convey that the two are priced consistently. In the opening exercise, the disagreement between the colleague and the market was a disagreement between a volatility of 25 percent and an implied volatility of 39 percent.\nThe best-known measure of implied volatility is the VIX index published by Cboe Global Markets, which is computed from the prices of options on the S&P 500 index expiring in about thirty days. Since 1990 it has usually stood between 12 and 25, and it exceeded 80 in October 2008 and in March 2020."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 12.2 — Implied volatility\nFrom a market price to a volatility, by bisection\nSet the market price of the three-month Northwind call with a strike of $30. The table shows the first eight steps of the search, and the figure locates the result on the curve that relates the price of the call to volatility."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 12.3 — Three-dimensional view\nThe value of a call as a function of the share price and the volatility\nThe surface shows the value of the three-month call with a strike of $30. At low volatility it lies on the lower bound of Result 8.1; as volatility increases it rises, most rapidly where the share price is near the strike. The gold curve is the value at the selected volatility. Drag to rotate; scroll or pinch to zoom.\nTable equivalent of this figure"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.6#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.6", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.6", "heading": "Volatility", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.6 Volatility", "body": "Concept check 12.2\nTwo call options on different shares both expire in three months. Option A costs $2.00 and has an implied volatility of 25 percent; option B costs $0.80 and has an implied volatility of 45 percent. In what sense can one be called more expensive than the other?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.7#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.7", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.7", "heading": "Dividends", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.7 Dividends", "body": "The formulas of Result 12.2 assume that the share pays no dividend. For a European option on a share that will pay known dividends, a simple adjustment suffices. The share price may be regarded as the sum of two components: a riskless component equal to the present value of the dividends to be paid during the life of the option, and a risky component consisting of the remainder. By the time the option expires the dividends will have been paid and the riskless component will no longer exist. The formulas therefore apply with \\(S_0\\) replaced by\n\\[ S_0-D, \\]\nwhere \\(D\\) is the present value of the dividends, discounted from their ex-dividend dates at the risk-free rate. This is the adjustment that Section 8.6 made to the bounds and to put–call parity. The volatility in the formula is, strictly, that of the risky component, which is slightly higher than the volatility of the share price as a whole."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.7#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.7", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.7", "heading": "Dividends", "kind": "example", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.7 Dividends", "body": "Worked example 12.3 — A call on a share that pays a dividend\nNorthwind will pay a dividend of $0.40 with an ex-dividend date in two months. Revalue the three-month European call with a strike of $30 of Worked example 12.1.\n1\nPresent value of the dividend. \\(D=0.40e^{-0.05\\times2/12}=\\$0.3967\\).\n2\nAdjusted share price. \\(28-0.3967=\\$27.6033\\).\n3\nFormula. \\(d_1=\\dfrac{\\ln(27.6033/30)+0.03151}{0.195}=-0.2654\\); \\(d_2=-0.4604\\); \\(N(d_1)=0.3954\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.3226\\).\n4\nCall. \\(c=27.6033\\times0.3954-29.6273\\times0.3226=10.913-9.558=\\$1.35\\). The dividend of $0.40 reduces the value of the call by $0.17 and, by parity, raises the value of the put by \\(0.40e^{-0.0083}-0.17=\\$0.23\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.7#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.7", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.7", "heading": "American options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.7 American options", "body": "By Result 8.3, an American call on a share that pays no dividend is worth the same as the European call, and the formula of Result 12.2 applies to it. When the share pays dividends, early exercise of the call may be optimal immediately before an ex-dividend date. A simple approximation due to Fischer Black is to compute the values of two European calls, one expiring at the original expiration date and one expiring immediately before the last ex-dividend date, and to take the greater of the two.\nFor American puts there is no exact formula, with or without dividends, because early exercise may be optimal at any time. They are valued numerically, by the binomial trees of Chapter 10 or by the methods of Chapter 16."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.8#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.8", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.8", "heading": "What the model does and does not claim", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.8 What the model does and does not claim", "body": "The model is the foundation of the modern derivatives industry, and it is used daily in forms little changed from those of 1973. Its claim should be stated precisely. It does not predict the price of a share, and it does not assert that the assumptions of Section 12.1 hold exactly. It asserts that if the share price follows geometric Brownian motion with a known volatility, an option can be replicated by trading in the share, and is therefore worth the cost of doing so.\nIn practice volatility is neither constant nor known, share prices occasionally jump, and trading is neither continuous nor free. Practitioners accommodate these facts within the framework of the model, principally by using a different implied volatility for each strike and expiration. That practice, and what it reveals about the distribution of share prices that the market in fact assumes, is the subject of Chapter 15.\nConcept check 12.3\nAnalysts revise their forecast of the return on a share from 8 percent to 14 percent per year. The share price, its volatility, and the risk-free rate are unchanged. What happens to the Black–Scholes–Merton value of a call on the share?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.8#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.8", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.8", "heading": "What the model does and does not claim", "kind": "notes", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.8 What the model does and does not claim", "body": "Historical note\nFischer Black and Myron Scholes completed their paper in 1970. It was rejected by two journals before it was published in the Journal of Political Economy in the spring of 1973, within weeks of the opening of the Chicago Board Options Exchange. Robert Merton, whose paper appeared in the same year, supplied the argument based on continuous replication and showed that the result did not depend on the equilibrium model of asset prices that Black and Scholes had originally used. Within two years traders on the exchange floor were using calculators programmed with the formula.\nScholes and Merton were awarded the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 1997; the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences recorded that Black, who had died in 1995, would have shared it. In 1998 the hedge fund Long-Term Capital Management, of which Scholes and Merton were partners, lost $4.6 billion, as recorded in Table 1.3. The loss arose from leverage and from the assumption that past relations between markets would persist, and not from the formula, but it has served since as a reminder of the distance between a model and the markets it describes."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.summary#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.summary", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.summary Summary", "body": "The model assumes a share price that follows geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility, continuous trading without costs, and a constant risk-free rate.\nA portfolio of the option and the share can be made riskless for an instant and must earn the risk-free rate. The resulting value of the option does not depend on the expected return on the share.\n\\(c=S_0N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)\\) and \\(p=Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2)-S_0N(-d_1)\\), with \\(d_1=[\\ln(S_0/K)+(r+\\sigma^{2}/2)T]/(\\sigma\\sqrt T)\\) and \\(d_2=d_1-\\sigma\\sqrt T\\).\n\\(N(d_2)\\) is the risk-neutral probability of exercise; \\(N(d_1)\\) is the delta of the call, the number of shares in its replicating portfolio.\nImplied volatility is the volatility at which the formula reproduces a market price. It is found by trial and is the unit in which option prices are compared.\nFor known dividends, \\(S_0\\) is replaced by \\(S_0-D\\). American puts, and American calls on shares that pay dividends, have no exact formula."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.exercises Exercises", "body": "12.1 A share that pays no dividend trades at $50 and has a volatility of 30 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Find the prices of six-month European options with a strike of $50.\n\\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.30\\times0.7071=0.2121\\). \\(d_1=[0+(0.04+0.045)\\times0.5]/0.2121=0.2003\\); \\(d_2=-0.0118\\). \\(N(d_1)=0.5794\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.4953\\). \\(Ke^{-rT}=50e^{-0.02}=49.010\\). \\(c=50\\times0.5794-49.010\\times0.4953=28.970-24.275=\\$4.70\\). \\(p=4.70+49.01-50=\\$3.71\\).\n12.2 A share that pays no dividend trades at $40 and has a volatility of 25 percent. The risk-free rate is 6 percent. Find the price of a nine-month European put with a strike of $45.\n\\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.25\\times0.8660=0.2165\\). \\(d_1=[\\ln(40/45)+(0.06+0.03125)\\times0.75]/0.2165=(-0.11778+0.06844)/0.2165=-0.2279\\); \\(d_2=-0.4444\\). \\(N(-d_1)=0.5901\\); \\(N(-d_2)=0.6716\\). \\(Ke^{-rT}=45e^{-0.045}=43.020\\). \\(p=43.020\\times0.6716-40\\times0.5901=28.893-23.606=\\$5.29\\).\n12.3 For a one-year European call with a strike of $95 on a share at $100 with a volatility of 20 percent and a risk-free rate of 5 percent, find (a) the price, (b) the risk-neutral probability of exercise, and (c) the composition of the replicating portfolio."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(d_1=[\\ln(100/95)+(0.05+0.02)\\times1]/0.20=(0.05129+0.07)/0.20=0.6065\\); \\(d_2=0.4065\\). \\(N(d_1)=0.7279\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.6578\\). \\(Ke^{-rT}=95e^{-0.05}=90.367\\). (a) \\(c=72.79-59.44=\\$13.35\\). (b) 65.8 percent. (c) 0.7279 shares, worth $72.79, financed by borrowing $59.44.\n12.4 Verify that the prices of the call and the put in Exercise 12.1 satisfy put–call parity, and explain why the call is worth more than the put although the option is at the money.\n\\(c+Ke^{-rT}=4.70+49.01=53.71\\); \\(p+S_0=3.71+50.00=53.71\\). By parity \\(c-p=S_0-Ke^{-rT}=50-49.01=\\$0.99\\): the holder of the call defers payment of the strike and earns interest on it, while the holder of the put defers its receipt.\n12.5 The Northwind call of Worked example 12.1 trades at $1.03. Using the fact that the formula gives $1.029 at a volatility of 30 percent, state the implied volatility, and say what a trader who believes the true volatility to be 39 percent should do."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.exercises Exercises", "body": "The implied volatility is 30 percent. On the trader's view the call is worth $1.52 and is cheap at $1.03. The trader should buy the call and, to profit from volatility and not from the direction of the share price, sell \\(N(d_1)\\) shares against it and adjust the hedge as the price changes, as Chapter 14 describes.\n12.6 A share trades at $28 and will pay a dividend of $0.40 with an ex-dividend date in two months. Using the result of Worked example 12.3 and put–call parity, find the value of the three-month European put with a strike of $30.\n\\(p=c+D+Ke^{-rT}-S_0=1.35+0.397+29.627-28.00=\\$3.38\\), against $3.14 without the dividend.\n12.7 Show that as \\(\\sigma\\) approaches zero the Black–Scholes–Merton price of a call approaches \\(\\max(S_0-Ke^{-rT},0)\\).\nWrite \\(d_1=[\\ln(S_0/K)+rT]/(\\sigma\\sqrt T)+\\sigma\\sqrt T/2\\). The numerator \\(\\ln(S_0/K)+rT\\) is positive when \\(S_0>Ke^{-rT}\\) and negative when \\(S_0<Ke^{-rT}\\). As \\(\\sigma\\to0\\), \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) therefore tend to \\(+\\infty\\) in the first case, so that \\(N(d_1)=N(d_2)=1\\) and \\(c\\to S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\); and to \\(-\\infty\\) in the second, so that both probabilities are zero and \\(c\\to0\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:12.exercises", "chapter": 12, "section": "12.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 12, The Black–Scholes–Merton Model — 12.exercises Exercises", "body": "12.8 ★ Show that \\(f=S\\) and \\(f=e^{rt}\\) are both solutions of the Black–Scholes–Merton differential equation, and interpret each.\nFor \\(f=S\\): \\(\\partial f/\\partial t=0\\), \\(\\partial f/\\partial S=1\\), \\(\\partial^{2}f/\\partial S^{2}=0\\); the left-hand side is \\(rS=rf\\). The share is itself a derivative of the share. For \\(f=e^{rt}\\): \\(\\partial f/\\partial t=re^{rt}\\) and the derivatives with respect to \\(S\\) are zero; the left-hand side is \\(re^{rt}=rf\\). This is a risk-free deposit. The equation is satisfied by every traded asset whose value depends on \\(S\\) and \\(t\\), and since it is linear, by every portfolio of them; the forward contract \\(S-Ke^{-r(T-t)}\\) is such a portfolio."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
